# Two pulses 12 gt apart toggle the bulb on, then off: the comparator gives ONE window (t2..t14).
# OPEN (K two cells from P): the window's rising edge extends P into the gap, its falling edge
# retracts it and pulls K one cell in (a pull-type piston accepts any pulse length to pull).
# CLOSE (K right in front of P): the window extends P, which pushes K back out (lands 2 gt later),
# then the falling edge 12 gt later is a NORMAL sticky retract (the pushed block is no longer
# moving): K is PULLED BACK. A window closes nothing; a pull-type piston needs a SHORT pulse
# (<= 2 gt: push and spit) on close, so an even link needs a second flip-flop (see memory-link).
design designs/gadgets/memory-window.txt
probe bulb 1 1 0
probe cmp 2 1 0
probe k4 4 1 0
probe k5 5 1 0
setblock 0 1 0 minecraft:redstone_block
expect bulb 1
quiet 2
setblock 0 1 0 minecraft:air
quiet 10
expect cmp 1
setblock 0 1 0 minecraft:redstone_block
expect bulb 0
quiet 2
setblock 0 1 0 minecraft:air
settle
expect cmp 0
expect_state 4 1 0 stone
expect_state 5 1 0 air
setblock 0 1 0 minecraft:redstone_block
expect bulb 1
quiet 2
setblock 0 1 0 minecraft:air
quiet 3
expect_state 5 1 0 stone
quiet 7
setblock 0 1 0 minecraft:redstone_block
expect bulb 0
quiet 2
setblock 0 1 0 minecraft:air
settle
expect_state 4 1 0 stone
expect_state 5 1 0 air
